      "text": "System Interface\n- Input : Ad click data from users\n- Output : Ad click metrics for advertisers",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "System Interface\n- Input : Ad click data from users\n- Output : Ad click metrics for advertisers",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "JDCPQbzfa1vGDVtiELMP4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -63897.971618226984,
      "y": -7133.337400017521,
      "width": 590.5995483398438,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1427354850,
      "version": 58,
      "versionNonce": 1464341858,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784608723661,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Data Flow\n- User clicks on an ad on a website.\n- The click is tracked and stored in the system.\n- The user is redirected to the advertiser's website.\n- Advertisers query the system for aggregated click metrics.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Data Flow\n- User clicks on an ad on a website.\n- The click is tracked and stored in the system.\n- The user is redirected to the advertiser's website.\n- Advertisers query the system for aggregated click metrics.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q9svZYiHONzM5SPpM8aba",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -65099.32296449523,
      "y": -6927.002266768086,
      "width": 1988.739013671875,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1906620578,
      "version": 1642,
      "versionNonce": 995566974,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784609424688,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n\nUsers can click on ads and be redirected to the target\n- Have an Ads placement service connected to Ads DB to place Ads on the website along with Ads metadata(redirectURL, image, text etc.).\n- What happens on a click - 2 options for redirects?\n\n1. Good Solution : Client Side redirect\n- user clicks on Ad, browser calls the Ad placement service and it returns the {adId, redirectURL}\n- Browser then redirects the user to the targetURL and in parallel calls our POST /click endpoint to track the click\nProblem : Users can grab the url off the page and navigate to it directly(probably some browser extension), bypassing our click tracking entirely.\nThis could lead to discrepancies in our click data and harder for advertisers to track performance of their ads\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Server side redirect\n- User clicks on Ad, we send a request to our server with {adId}\n- Our server calls the Ad Placement Service which calls its Ads DB and returns the redirectUrl agains the adId\n- We return a 302 redirect to browser, and proceed to store our click data\n- This way we ensure no bypassing happens, we can also append some additional parameters to URL, which will help advertiser to track the source of the click when upon redirect they reach their website.\nProblem : Added complexity, added a bit of latency",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n\nUsers can click on ads and be redirected to the target\n- Have an Ads placement service connected to Ads DB to place Ads on the website along with Ads metadata(redirectURL, image, text etc.).\n- What happens on a click - 2 options for redirects?\n\n1. Good Solution : Client Side redirect\n- user clicks on Ad, browser calls the Ad placement service and it returns the {adId, redirectURL}\n- Browser then redirects the user to the targetURL and in parallel calls our POST /click endpoint to track the click\nProblem : Users can grab the url off the page and navigate to it directly(probably some browser extension), bypassing our click tracking entirely.\nThis could lead to discrepancies in our click data and harder for advertisers to track performance of their ads\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Server side redirect\n- User clicks on Ad, we send a request to our server with {adId}\n- Our server calls the Ad Placement Service which calls its Ads DB and returns the redirectUrl agains the adId\n- We return a 302 redirect to browser, and proceed to store our click data\n- This way we ensure no bypassing happens, we can also append some additional parameters to URL, which will help advertiser to track the source of the click when upon redirect they reach their website.\nProblem : Added complexity, added a bit of latency",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "y50cZJcnjYwVjwY52UJOH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -65092.3619512269,
      "y": -6359.663529214891,
      "width": 2376.278076171875,
      "height": 1200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744673954,
      "version": 3078,
      "versionNonce": 1030105378,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784618572186,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Advertisers can query Ad click metrics over time at 1 minute intervals\n\n1. Bad Solution : Store and Query from the same DB\n- When click comes in, we simply store that event in our DB\n\n        EventId        Ad Id        User Id        Timestamp\n            1                123           456         1640000000\n\n- When analytics required run below query\n        SELECT\n          COUNT(*) as total_clicks,\n          COUNT(DISTINCT UserId) as unique_users\n        FROM ClickEvents\n        WHERE AdId = 123\n          AND Timestamp BETWEEN 1640000000 AND 1640000001\n\nProblem : At scale this solution will not work, GROUP BY query is extremely slow, low latency requirement not met\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Separate Analytics Dashboard with Batch Processing\n- Have a separate DB that stores pre-aggregated data and Analytics service queries that DB\n- Ad comes in, we store raw click event in DB like before, Then in batches, we process raw events and aggregate them in separate Analytics DB\n- The schema of this aggregated DB looks like\n\n        Ad Id        Minute Timestamp        Unique Clicks\n          123            1640000000                 100\n\n- To pull this off, we need high throughput write DB Cassandra, not optimised for range queries or aggregations thats where Apache Spark comes in, Its a compute engine \n- Spark is a distributed computing engine optimised for aggregation and range querying of data, handles large volume.\n- 10k clicks/s and batch processing every 5 min = 3Mil events/batch ~ 300MB data/batch not that huge could be done on a single machine but spark becomes useful when the data grows, Uses MapReduce to solve this.\n- Using map-reduce, Spark will read the raw events in parallel chunks, aggregate them by ad ID and minute timestamp, and then write the aggregated data to our analytics database.\n- For analytics DB, we need something that's optimised for Reads and Aggregations.\n- Online Analytical Processing(OLAP) DB like Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery\n- OLAP DB use columnar storage formats to make aggregation queries(COUNT, SUM, AVG) over million of rows extremely fast compared to row-based DB.\n- Why not use a TimeSeries DB like InfluxDB or TimescaleDB? - These works well when we have low cardinality, our system have millions of AdIds, advertisers will want to slice data by multiple dimensions(device type, geography, campaign etc.)\n     OLAP databases handle this high-cardinality, multi-dimensional querying much better.\n     But if requirements are truly limited to simple time-range queries over a single metic, TSDB is a good choice.\n- Steps : \n    - The ad click is sent to the click processor service.\n    - The click processor service writes the event to our event store.\n    - Every N minutes, a cron job kicks off a spark job that reads the events from the event store and aggregates them.\n    - The aggregated data is stored in an OLAP database for querying.\n    - Advertisers can query the OLAP database to get metrics on their ads.\n\nProblem : Batch processing introduces a 5 min delay, not real time anymore, Handling traffic spikes is difficult now, if sudden burst of clicks received, the next batch have to process much larger data causing it to\nrun longer than the batch interval itself. This introduces a cascading delay and advertisers see increasingly stale data right when they needed the most accurate one(a big ad launch event)\nAdding a queue b/w click processor and Cassandra would solve the write spikes but we still have the latency problem\nWe need a realtime stream processing engine.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Advertisers can query Ad click metrics over time at 1 minute intervals\n\n1. Bad Solution : Store and Query from the same DB\n- When click comes in, we simply store that event in our DB\n\n        EventId        Ad Id        User Id        Timestamp\n            1                123           456         1640000000\n\n- When analytics required run below query\n        SELECT\n          COUNT(*) as total_clicks,\n          COUNT(DISTINCT UserId) as unique_users\n        FROM ClickEvents\n        WHERE AdId = 123\n          AND Timestamp BETWEEN 1640000000 AND 1640000001\n\nProblem : At scale this solution will not work, GROUP BY query is extremely slow, low latency requirement not met\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Separate Analytics Dashboard with Batch Processing\n- Have a separate DB that stores pre-aggregated data and Analytics service queries that DB\n- Ad comes in, we store raw click event in DB like before, Then in batches, we process raw events and aggregate them in separate Analytics DB\n- The schema of this aggregated DB looks like\n\n        Ad Id        Minute Timestamp        Unique Clicks\n          123            1640000000                 100\n\n- To pull this off, we need high throughput write DB Cassandra, not optimised for range queries or aggregations thats where Apache Spark comes in, Its a compute engine \n- Spark is a distributed computing engine optimised for aggregation and range querying of data, handles large volume.\n- 10k clicks/s and batch processing every 5 min = 3Mil events/batch ~ 300MB data/batch not that huge could be done on a single machine but spark becomes useful when the data grows, Uses MapReduce to solve this.\n- Using map-reduce, Spark will read the raw events in parallel chunks, aggregate them by ad ID and minute timestamp, and then write the aggregated data to our analytics database.\n- For analytics DB, we need something that's optimised for Reads and Aggregations.\n- Online Analytical Processing(OLAP) DB like Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery\n- OLAP DB use columnar storage formats to make aggregation queries(COUNT, SUM, AVG) over million of rows extremely fast compared to row-based DB.\n- Why not use a TimeSeries DB like InfluxDB or TimescaleDB? - These works well when we have low cardinality, our system have millions of AdIds, advertisers will want to slice data by multiple dimensions(device type, geography, campaign etc.)\n     OLAP databases handle this high-cardinality, multi-dimensional querying much better.\n     But if requirements are truly limited to simple time-range queries over a single metic, TSDB is a good choice.\n- Steps : \n    - The ad click is sent to the click processor service.\n    - The click processor service writes the event to our event store.\n    - Every N minutes, a cron job kicks off a spark job that reads the events from the event store and aggregates them.\n    - The aggregated data is stored in an OLAP database for querying.\n    - Advertisers can query the OLAP database to get metrics on their ads.\n\nProblem : Batch processing introduces a 5 min delay, not real time anymore, Handling traffic spikes is difficult now, if sudden burst of clicks received, the next batch have to process much larger data causing it to\nrun longer than the batch interval itself. This introduces a cascading delay and advertisers see increasingly stale data right when they needed the most accurate one(a big ad launch event)\nAdding a queue b/w click processor and Cassandra would solve the write spikes but we still have the latency problem\nWe need a realtime stream processing engine.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
