      "updated": 1784613488507,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Great Solution : Real-time Analytics with Stream Processing\n- We need to process events as they come in rather than a batching job\n- When a click comes in our click processing service will immediately write the event to a stream like Kafka or Kinesis.\n- Stream Processor like Flink or Spark Streaming will read from the stream and aggregate them in real time.\n- You might argue why not have regular Kafka consumers keep polling from stream and have a running count and flush to OLAP DB every minute?\n    - That's an okay answer for mid-level but Flink provides many features that are painful to build yourself.\n    - Flink handles windowed aggregations with event-time semantics(so out-of-order events land in the correct minute bucket)\n    - Flink handles watermarks that know when it's safe to close a window\n    - Exactly-once processing guarantees\n    - Built-in fault tolerance with state recovery\n    Rolling your own version of all that on top of raw Kafka consumers is doable but error prone\n- Flink works by keeping a running count of click totals in memory and updating them as new events come in.\n- We use event time(when click actually occurred) rather than processing time(when Flink receives the event) to ensure accurate aggregations even when events arrive out of order\n- Flink uses watermarks to track event time progress and handle late-arriving events.\n- When we reach the end of a window we flush aggregated data to OLAP DB\n- Steps are : \n    - The click processor service writes the event to a stream like Kafka or Kinesis.\n    - A stream processor like Flink or Spark Streaming reads the events from the stream and aggregates them in real-time.\n    - The aggregated data is stored in our OLAP database for querying.\n    - Advertisers can query the OLAP database to get metrics on their ads in near real-time.\n\nProblems : Flink needs to be further optimized for periodic flushing(couple of sec) - discussed in deep dives",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Great Solution : Real-time Analytics with Stream Processing\n- We need to process events as they come in rather than a batching job\n- When a click comes in our click processing service will immediately write the event to a stream like Kafka or Kinesis.\n- Stream Processor like Flink or Spark Streaming will read from the stream and aggregate them in real time.\n- You might argue why not have regular Kafka consumers keep polling from stream and have a running count and flush to OLAP DB every minute?\n    - That's an okay answer for mid-level but Flink provides many features that are painful to build yourself.\n    - Flink handles windowed aggregations with event-time semantics(so out-of-order events land in the correct minute bucket)\n    - Flink handles watermarks that know when it's safe to close a window\n    - Exactly-once processing guarantees\n    - Built-in fault tolerance with state recovery\n    Rolling your own version of all that on top of raw Kafka consumers is doable but error prone\n- Flink works by keeping a running count of click totals in memory and updating them as new events come in.\n- We use event time(when click actually occurred) rather than processing time(when Flink receives the event) to ensure accurate aggregations even when events arrive out of order\n- Flink uses watermarks to track event time progress and handle late-arriving events.\n- When we reach the end of a window we flush aggregated data to OLAP DB\n- Steps are : \n    - The click processor service writes the event to a stream like Kafka or Kinesis.\n    - A stream processor like Flink or Spark Streaming reads the events from the stream and aggregates them in real-time.\n    - The aggregated data is stored in our OLAP database for querying.\n    - Advertisers can query the OLAP database to get metrics on their ads in near real-time.\n\nProblems : Flink needs to be further optimized for periodic flushing(couple of sec) - discussed in deep dives",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
