    },
    {
      "id": "TpgW9Vc_9dHoe9qvWMQv8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -62569.59800631082,
      "y": -7438.170175956834,
      "width": 2349.658203125,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aD",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 734982846,
      "version": 1619,
      "versionNonce": 722990910,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784618586495,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to scale to support 10k click/sec?\n- Horizontally scale Click Processor Service, add LB in front.\n- Kinesis has a limit of 1MB/s or 1000 records/s per shard, need to add some sharding by AdId, stream processors read from multiple shards in parallel and are independent of each other(all events for a given AdId will be in the same shard)\n- Scale Flink to process a shard independently so that it has to process data for the same AdId together.\n- OLAP like SnowFlake or BigQuery scale automatically, for self managed solutions like ClickHouse, shard by AdvertiserId so all data for a given advertiser lives on the same node, making query for that advertiser's ads faster\n- With all this we should be able to handle peak of 10k queries/sec\n\nHot Shards\n- Very popular Ads getting a lot of clicks will end up in the same shard, its overwhelmed now, increased latency, and can even cause data loss.\n- Need to further partition the data, append partition key with a random number. Partition key becomes \"AdId:0-N\" where N is the number of additional partitions for that AdId\n- When writing to OLAP, Flink strips the suffix and uses the original AdId so that while Aggregation in OLAP data lives on the same partition. Alternatively store sub-keys and aggregate at query time.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to scale to support 10k click/sec?\n- Horizontally scale Click Processor Service, add LB in front.\n- Kinesis has a limit of 1MB/s or 1000 records/s per shard, need to add some sharding by AdId, stream processors read from multiple shards in parallel and are independent of each other(all events for a given AdId will be in the same shard)\n- Scale Flink to process a shard independently so that it has to process data for the same AdId together.\n- OLAP like SnowFlake or BigQuery scale automatically, for self managed solutions like ClickHouse, shard by AdvertiserId so all data for a given advertiser lives on the same node, making query for that advertiser's ads faster\n- With all this we should be able to handle peak of 10k queries/sec\n\nHot Shards\n- Very popular Ads getting a lot of clicks will end up in the same shard, its overwhelmed now, increased latency, and can even cause data loss.\n- Need to further partition the data, append partition key with a random number. Partition key becomes \"AdId:0-N\" where N is the number of additional partitions for that AdId\n- When writing to OLAP, Flink strips the suffix and uses the original AdId so that while Aggregation in OLAP data lives on the same partition. Alternatively store sub-keys and aggregate at query time.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8fw7MLjhFRoxCqKIUPAdg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -62560.58002893178,
      "y": -7066.806660047123,
      "width": 1888.878662109375,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2138009534,
      "version": 1911,
      "versionNonce": 902979454,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784618586495,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure we don't loose any data?\n- We already are using Kafka/Kinesis which are already fault tolerant and highly available, have replications etc. to meet distributed system needs\n- We also enable persistent storage that means even if data is consumed by Flink and acknowledged its removed from the stream but still stored for a certain period of time default 7 days.\n- This allows Flink to pick back on the lost data it Flink goes down.\n- Flink also supports CheckPointing where it periodically writes its state to S3, it Flink goes down it just reads the last checkpoint and resume from there\n    - Only useful when aggregation window is large(day, month etc) but we only have a 1min aggregation window easily reconstructible from Kafka\n\n\nReconciliation\n- If data lost we loose money, our data needs to be highly accurate, tough because guaranteeing correctness with low latency is tricky, need to introduce periodic reconciliation to achieve this.\n- Transient processing error in Flink, bad code pushes, out-of-order events in streams etc could lead to slight inaccuracies.\n- At the end of the stream processors, we dump raw click event data to S3, Kafka(Kafka Connect S3 Sink Connector) & Kinesis(Kinesis Data Firehose) both supports this.\n- Then apply that Apache Spark solution with 1Hr or 1Day batches as a reconciliation solution, and compare data of Stream processing and this and see if they match, if not we fix it.\n- This gives us best of both worlds - Lambda Architecture(Processing real time - the Flink solution) + ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure we don't loose any data?\n- We already are using Kafka/Kinesis which are already fault tolerant and highly available, have replications etc. to meet distributed system needs\n- We also enable persistent storage that means even if data is consumed by Flink and acknowledged its removed from the stream but still stored for a certain period of time default 7 days.\n- This allows Flink to pick back on the lost data it Flink goes down.\n- Flink also supports CheckPointing where it periodically writes its state to S3, it Flink goes down it just reads the last checkpoint and resume from there\n    - Only useful when aggregation window is large(day, month etc) but we only have a 1min aggregation window easily reconstructible from Kafka\n\n\nReconciliation\n- If data lost we loose money, our data needs to be highly accurate, tough because guaranteeing correctness with low latency is tricky, need to introduce periodic reconciliation to achieve this.\n- Transient processing error in Flink, bad code pushes, out-of-order events in streams etc could lead to slight inaccuracies.\n- At the end of the stream processors, we dump raw click event data to S3, Kafka(Kafka Connect S3 Sink Connector) & Kinesis(Kinesis Data Firehose) both supports this.\n- Then apply that Apache Spark solution with 1Hr or 1Day batches as a reconciliation solution, and compare data of Stream processing and this and see if they match, if not we fix it.\n- This gives us best of both worlds - Lambda Architecture(Processing real time - the Flink solution) + ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "MEd4x5cQiEQHNqVH7XgJs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -62556.09943522558,
      "y": -6640.801908019566,
      "width": 2092.118896484375,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1081504894,
      "version": 2857,
      "versionNonce": 1644998690,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784619351405,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to prevent abuse from users clicks on Ads multiple time(Ad Click Idempotency)?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Add userId to click event payload\n- Requiring users to be logged in before they can see an Ad, this allows us to have their userId extracted from JWT or session cookie and we can dedup based on userId and adId\n- It needs to be done before the Ad data is put into stream because we need to dedup across aggregation window, If a duplicate click comes in on either side of a minute boundary, it will be counted as two clicks.\nProblems : Can't expect users to log in. this approach only allows us that we count only one click for any given user on any given ad, but we don't want this exactly\nWhat when we intentionally want to show same add to the same user multiple times over an extended period - So the problem to solve is One Click Per Ad Instance.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Generate a Unique Impression Id\n- Ad Placement Service generates a unique Impression Id for each Ad instance shown to each user\n- Every time an ad is rendered in a user's browser, it gets a fresh impression ID - So if 1000 users see the same Nike ad, that's 1000 different impression IDs.\n- This impression ID would be sent to the browser along with the ad and will serve as an idempotency key.\n- When the user clicks on the ad, the browser sends the impression ID along with the click data and we dedup on it.\n- What is an Impression? - An ad impression is a metric that represents the display of an ad on a web page. It is counted when the ad is fetched from its source and displayed on the user's screen. \n  If we showed the same user the same ad multiple times in multiple places, then each of these placements is a new impression.\n- How to dedup\n    - Not possible at flink layer as we need to dedup across aggregation windows, i.e. if a duplicate click comes in on either side of a minute boundary, it will be counted as two clicks.\n    - We dedup before we put the click event to stream\n    - When click comes in, check if the impression Id exists in a Redis cache, if yes ignore it else add to cache and put it to stream\n    - Problem is a malicious user can send bunch of fake clicks with falsified impressionId as they all are unique and we count them all - not good\n    - Sign the ImpressionId with AdId using HMAC with a secret key before sending it to browser, when we receive it through click event we verify both\n    - Prevents attackers from harvesting valid signed impressionIds and replaying them against different ads.\n    - HMAC verification is extremely fast(microsec) - Its hash computation with no asymmetric cryptography involved so no latency added.\n\nProblems : Cache needs to be managed properly should not add latency to our service.\n    100M * 16 bytes(128 bits) = 1.6GB cache data - very low\n\n\n\nNote : we currently are only aggregation minute data which is extremely fast but what if we need aggregated data for days, weeks, months, years etc. Then we need to pre-aggregate these data in OLAP DB\nCreate a new table to store data at higher level of granularity via some night cron job - Very Common Technique",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to prevent abuse from users clicks on Ads multiple time(Ad Click Idempotency)?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Add userId to click event payload\n- Requiring users to be logged in before they can see an Ad, this allows us to have their userId extracted from JWT or session cookie and we can dedup based on userId and adId\n- It needs to be done before the Ad data is put into stream because we need to dedup across aggregation window, If a duplicate click comes in on either side of a minute boundary, it will be counted as two clicks.\nProblems : Can't expect users to log in. this approach only allows us that we count only one click for any given user on any given ad, but we don't want this exactly\nWhat when we intentionally want to show same add to the same user multiple times over an extended period - So the problem to solve is One Click Per Ad Instance.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Generate a Unique Impression Id\n- Ad Placement Service generates a unique Impression Id for each Ad instance shown to each user\n- Every time an ad is rendered in a user's browser, it gets a fresh impression ID - So if 1000 users see the same Nike ad, that's 1000 different impression IDs.\n- This impression ID would be sent to the browser along with the ad and will serve as an idempotency key.\n- When the user clicks on the ad, the browser sends the impression ID along with the click data and we dedup on it.\n- What is an Impression? - An ad impression is a metric that represents the display of an ad on a web page. It is counted when the ad is fetched from its source and displayed on the user's screen. \n  If we showed the same user the same ad multiple times in multiple places, then each of these placements is a new impression.\n- How to dedup\n    - Not possible at flink layer as we need to dedup across aggregation windows, i.e. if a duplicate click comes in on either side of a minute boundary, it will be counted as two clicks.\n    - We dedup before we put the click event to stream\n    - When click comes in, check if the impression Id exists in a Redis cache, if yes ignore it else add to cache and put it to stream\n    - Problem is a malicious user can send bunch of fake clicks with falsified impressionId as they all are unique and we count them all - not good\n    - Sign the ImpressionId with AdId using HMAC with a secret key before sending it to browser, when we receive it through click event we verify both\n    - Prevents attackers from harvesting valid signed impressionIds and replaying them against different ads.\n    - HMAC verification is extremely fast(microsec) - Its hash computation with no asymmetric cryptography involved so no latency added.\n\nProblems : Cache needs to be managed properly should not add latency to our service.\n    100M * 16 bytes(128 bits) = 1.6GB cache data - very low\n\n\n\nNote : we currently are only aggregation minute data which is extremely fast but what if we need aggregated data for days, weeks, months, years etc. Then we need to pre-aggregate these data in OLAP DB\nCreate a new table to store data at higher level of granularity via some night cron job - Very Common Technique",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
